TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Preliminary Report (Part 1)","overview":"Plan a simple space mission that benefits Australia and present preliminary design work to guide the full mission assignment. Explain the mission concept and rationale, plan how the assignment will be completed, identify information sources and software, and show evidence of technical progress."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Mission concept and rationale — What will it do, who are the users, why is it needed?","weight":40,"max":40,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"A clear and complete description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Clearly relates required mission function to users and their needs. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Sufficient description of mission goals, objectives, and requirements. Identification of users and their needs. Descriptions feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"A poor description with mission goals, objectives and requirements unclear. Insufficient identification of users and their needs."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Project plan — How you plan to complete the assignment, including either a Gantt chart or a timetable and information sources you plan to use","weight":30,"max":30,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The plan for project completion is robust and has provision for project variations and contingencies. The plan fits within the narrative set out by the mission concept – the student makes clear why the plan is developed this way in the context of the mission. Appropriate references to information sources are included."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Plan is presented but lacks some detail. However, the plan is enough to believe that the project is feasible."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Plan is not included or does not have sufficient detail to demonstrate that the project can be completed."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Preliminary progress — Technical content and evidence of sufficient work done to date","weight":10,"max":10,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Good preliminary set of results included with brief description and/or interpretation. Clearly on track for completion."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Minor work completed on the project. Results description or interpretation may be insufficient."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful work completed."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":20,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical structure. The report is very easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Mission Concept & Rationale"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan; Preliminary Progress"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Project Plan"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Information sources"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Preliminary Progress"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The central concept assumes visible-band satellite imagery can detect coral colour changes through water at 10 m ground sample distance. Water depth, turbidity, atmospheric effects, cloud cover, spectral bands and water-column correction can strongly limit this; the report does not establish sensor feasibility or validate the requirement against the cited airborne method."},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","issue":"The 60 km swath estimate assumes 6,000 across-track pixels at 10 m sampling, but no sensor field of view or instrument design supports that assumption. Five swaths could geometrically span 250 km, but the stated approximately 2,700 km equatorial ground-track separation means adjacent orbital passes will not simply provide those neighbouring strips; revisit and coverage need orbit simulation."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The statement that oceans have become 30 percent more acidic is imprecise: the commonly cited increase is in hydrogen-ion concentration, not a linear percentage change in a general measure of acidity."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","issue":"The introduction mentions bathymetry as a potential product, but the objectives and preliminary design focus on bleaching imagery. Bathymetry would require its own performance requirements and feasibility assessment."}],"overall":"A strong, well-structured preliminary report with a clear Australian mission rationale, credible project plan and meaningful early orbit work. The main unresolved engineering risk is whether the proposed optical imaging approach can deliver reliable reef-scale bleaching detection, alongside the orbit coverage and sensor-swath trade.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":40.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose, need, operational objectives, intended operator and user groups are clearly connected. Quantified revisit, resolution, delivery and lifetime requirements add useful specificity, and the rationale is supported by sources. The concept would be stronger with validation that satellite visible-band imagery at the proposed resolution can distinguish bleaching through the water column.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Give reef managers direct, repeatable observations of coral colour over the whole Great Barrier Reef, so that bleaching is detected within days instead of after a seasonal aerial survey."},{"where":"Mission Concept & Rationale","quote":"Its customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority, which needs up-to-date bleaching maps to decide where to send survey and response teams"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":27.0,"max":30.0,"reasoning":"The Gantt chart gives a sequenced schedule, and the narrative explains dependencies between imaging, orbit, ground control and communications. Planned information sources are identified, and specific schedule, software-access and coverage contingencies are provided. Some work packages remain broad, but the plan is robust and feasible.","evidence":[{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"STK was chosen because its access and coverage tools answer the two questions this mission depends on"},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Week 10 is float as well as editing time: no new design work is planned in it."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The submission contains useful preliminary orbit calculations, a coverage-versus-swath assessment, an interpretation of the main design challenge and a stated STK scenario. The work demonstrates progress. The coverage inference is preliminary and does not establish revisit performance; sensor feasibility and a more complete orbit-coverage analysis remain open.","evidence":[{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"Period | T = 2π√(a³/μ) | 5,801 s = 96.7 min"},{"where":"Preliminary Progress","quote":"A first STK scenario with this orbit has been set up, and its period agrees with the hand calculation."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, readable prose, clearly labelled tables and figure, and a substantial reference list with in-text citations. A few informal phrases and some uneven source detail keep it short of exemplary professional polish, but presentation is clear and consistent overall.","evidence":[{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Project Plan","quote":"Figure 1: Project Gantt chart, Weeks 2 to 10 (gold = completed, blue = planned, red lines = report deadlines)."},{"where":"References","quote":"[7] J. R. Wertz, D. F. Everett, and J. J. Puschell, Eds., Space Mission Engineering: The New SMAD."}]}]}
